  "narrative": {
    "text": "A small thumbnail of a target health supply chain architecture diagram, shown in the 'Year 3 and 4 – Target architecture maturity to next level' cell of Fig. 21, 'Example of outputs from this chapter', in the Defining the DHSC architecture chapter. The diagram is arranged in labelled boxes: Shared services with foundational components such as Facility Registry and Product Registry; Data services with Analytics (BI reports, Data warehouse) and Traceability (Track and Trace, Product Verification); an Interoperability layer of enabling components such as Authentication, Encryption, Orchestration, Transformation, Translation and Validation; and Health supply chain systems including Forecasting and Planning, Order Management, Warehouse Management, Cost Management, Procurement, Supplier and Contract Management, Transportation Management and Dispensing. To the right, Digital ecosystem and External systems boxes list items such as Quality systems, Health management information systems, Regulatory systems, Finance and insurance systems, Global platform, Manufacturer systems and Data repositories. At this size the labels are only partly legible.",
